WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Costco's Big Secret: It Doesn't Play by the Rules

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.000
To say that Costco has a legion of fans is no exaggeration.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:24.000
More than 51 million people ponied up more than $3 billion in membership fees in 2023 to get access to Costco and its mega-discounts.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Zachary Crockett, a reporter at the daily business newsletter, looked closely at what's driving the cultish devotion towards the global chain,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
and why the retailer is doing so well even as consumers do more and more of their shopping online.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.000
What is Crockett's take?

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Costco has succeeded by flipping conventional business wisdom on its head by putting its customers' interests ahead of its shareholders'.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:11.000
That's evident in its profit margin. By Crockett's calculations, the average markup at Costco is 11% compared to the 25% to 50% average markup in the retail industry.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.000
And the price being marked up is often lower than what consumers will find elsewhere.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.000
This is thanks to Costco's purchasing power.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.000
Take, for example, a pound of wagyu beef.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:36.000
That pound of beef, which costs Costco $100, would theoretically cost a Costco shopper $111.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:50.000
However, that same pound at a regular grocery store would cost the consumer $140, due to a higher markup and a higher purchase price for the grocer.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:58.000
As an example of just how little Costco tries to squeeze its customers, consider this anecdote.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:08.000
At a time when Calvin Klein jeans typically cost around $50, Costco sold them for $29.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:14.000
Based on their increased sales volume, they could then secure a better purchasing deal.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.000
But instead of pocketing the extra profit, they dropped the price further, to $22.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Costco will even work with vendors to redo its products and processes so as to lower the price, with the savings going to the customer rather than their bottom line.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:45.000
This strategy toward favoring the customer also coincides with a similar attitude of just how well Costco treats its employees.
